    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one framework for the structure and limits of government power. Separation of powers sets the stage for asking which branch, or which level of government, may act at all, and judicial review is the actual mechanism courts use to police that boundary once a genuine dispute arrives. Justiciability is the threshold gate that decides whether a court will even reach that question, and the level of scrutiny that then applies determines how hard the government actually has to work to justify what it did once the gate is cleared. The First Amendment, due process, and the Takings Clause are three of the clearest individual rights that scrutiny framework was built to protect, each guarding a different, specific kind of government overreach. The Commerce Clause and preemption together define how far federal power reaches, both into new regulatory territory and into territory the states might otherwise have occupied on their own. Administrative deference, or its recent, considerable absence after Loper Bright, closes the lecture because it is where all of this structural theory meets the actual daily grind of a federal agency writing a rule and a court deciding how much independent scrutiny that rule now actually deserves. Notice how often you had to ask the same underlying question across all ten concepts: who actually holds the power to decide, and how far does a court let that decision-maker go before stepping in to correct it. That question runs from the President's use of emergency authority all the way down to a fisheries agency billing a fishing boat for its own onboard observer, and the answer has shifted meaningfully within your own professional lifetime, most dramatically with Loper Bright's rejection of automatic deference in 2024. As you carry this framework into practice, resist treating any of these ten doctrines as settled once and for all; federalism, scrutiny, and deference in particular are all still actively moving, and the version of each doctrine you learned even a few years ago may already be measurably out of date. Whatever specific dispute lands on your desk, start by locating it on this map, structural or rights-based, threshold or merits, judicial or administrative, before you reach for the substantive arguments, because knowing where a dispute sits within this framework will usually tell you which arguments are actually worth making at all.",
